Here’s what was added and how it works.

### Approach

Next.js treats **`export const dynamic = 'force-dynamic'`** on a route segment as the explicit way to **always render on the server per request**, so the page is not statically prerendered at build time. That matches “dynamic data without cookies/headers.”

That flag belongs on the **route** (`page.tsx` / `layout.tsx`), not on an arbitrary child file, so it stays on `app/page.tsx` while the UI lives in a separate server component.

### `ServerTimestamp` (server component)

```1:14:/vercel/sandbox/app/components/ServerTimestamp.tsx
export default function ServerTimestamp() {
  const at = new Date()

  return (
    <time dateTime={at.toISOString()}>
      {at.toLocaleString(undefined, {
        dateStyle: 'full',
        timeStyle: 'long',
      })}
    </time>
  )
}
```

### Home page: force dynamic + render

```1:16:/vercel/sandbox/app/page.tsx
import ServerTimestamp from './components/ServerTimestamp'

export const dynamic = 'force-dynamic'

export default function Home() {
  return (
    <main>
      <h1>Server time</h1>
      <p>
        Current server timestamp (new value on every request):{' '}
        <ServerTimestamp />
      </p>
    </main>
  )
}
```

`next build` reports `/` as **Dynamic** (`ƒ`), so it is rendered on demand, not as static HTML frozen at build time. Refreshing in production will show a new timestamp each time.

